## Plugin authoring

If the user asks for a package manager that no official plugin covers (e.g.
`pacman`, `apk`, `flatpak`, a custom internal tool), you can scaffold a local
plugin with `homeos plugin add --local <name>`. That writes a
`plugins/<name>/plugin.yml` and a full set of template files
(`install.sh.tmpl`, `install.ps1.tmpl`, etc.) for the user — or you — to
fill in.

The mechanics are a parameter list in `plugin.yml`, plus templates that use
`{{ key }}` substitution. The README's "Plugin Development Guide" section
covers the details and is the authoritative source. When in doubt, read the
README rather than improvising — plugin schema changes happen between
versions.

## Maintaining the repository README

The homeos data directory is a Git repository, and like any other Git
repo it benefits from a `README.md` at the root explaining what it
manages. This README is **for humans** — the user, future contributors,
anyone who clones the repo onto a new machine.

`homeos` does not generate this file. The definition lives here in
`AGENTS.md`, and you (the agent) maintain it. That way the conventions
evolve with whichever homeos version is installed on each machine, with
no stale skeleton lingering from an older release.

### When to create it

On the first mutation (plugin add, package add, etc.) in a session
where `<data_dir>/README.md` does not exist, create it using the
structure below. Include it in the first commit. The title is the
fixed string `Homeos Repo` — do not prompt the user, do not personalize
with the hostname (this file gets pushed to remote, hostname leakage
should be avoided).

### When to update it

Whenever you mutate `homeos.yml`, refresh the corresponding section of
the README in the same commit as the mutation. The user does not need
to remember to update it — that is part of your job.

### Structure

```markdown
# homeos-repo

This repository is the source of truth for the user's software setup,
managed by [homeos](https://github.com/hainet50b/homeos). To restore on
any machine:

​```sh
homeos init <this repo's URL>
homeos apply
​```

## Packages

(agent-maintained: one bullet per package in `homeos.yml`, with a short
purpose line)

- **neovim** — primary text editor.
- **rustup** — Rust toolchain manager.

## Plugins

(agent-maintained: one bullet per registered plugin, briefly describing
what it provides)

- **dnf** — Fedora's system package manager.

## Notes

(user-owned. The agent reads this section for context but never
modifies it.)

---

*Managed by [homeos](https://github.com/hainet50b/homeos).*
```

### Ownership boundary

You own the **Packages** and **Plugins** sections; keep them in sync
with `homeos.yml`. The **Notes** section is the user's: read it for
context, but do not edit or reorder its content. Do not rename or
restructure the file.

If the user manually edits the Packages or Plugins sections, your next
update will overwrite their edits. When you first create the README,
mention this to the user and suggest they put custom prose in
**Notes**, which is preserved.

## Local customizations

The user may keep an `AGENTS.local.md` file next to this file in the homeos
data directory. If it exists, read it after this file and treat its
instructions as additional guidance specific to the user's setup — naming
conventions for their packages, preferred plugins, sensitive operations to
double-check, machines they're explicitly opted out of, and so on. The
instructions in `AGENTS.local.md` extend the ones here; they do not contradict
the safety rules (still always dry-run before mutating, still always commit
after a change).

`homeos` never reads, modifies, or removes `AGENTS.local.md`. It is the
user's file, hand-written. Treat its absence as "no extra instructions";
treat its presence as "read this carefully before acting".
